program            = 006_enum_definitions
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Enumerator value assignment, gaps, negative enumerators and the int range edges, with switch lowering over a sparse label set
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
No signed overflow occurs anywhere, including at the int range edges. B_LOW is written as
-2147483647 - 1 rather than as -2147483648 precisely so that every intermediate value is an
int-typed constant within int range: the literal 2147483648 would not fit in int and would
acquire a wider type, whereas 2147483647 fits and subtracting 1 from its negation reaches the
int minimum with no step outside the range. B_HIGH is the int maximum written directly. Both
edge values are only printed, never used as arithmetic operands, so no computation approaches
either edge. The arithmetic that is performed uses only small enumerators: COLOR_BLUE plus S_B
is 13 and S_D minus S_A is 11, both far from the range edges. The cast (enum sparse)selector
converts the value 20, which is exactly the enumerator S_C and therefore within the
enumeration's value range, so the conversion is value preserving. classify handles all four
enumerators with case labels and additionally returns -1 after the switch, so its control flow
is well defined for any argument value and control can never fall off the end of the function.
Every object read is initialized: selector has an initializer and folded, runtime and arith are
all assigned at their declarations. No shift and no pointer arithmetic is performed. No object
is modified twice between sequence points, and no printf call receives a side-effecting
argument -- both classify results are assigned to locals on separate statements first, so
unspecified evaluation order cannot affect the output. There is no aliasing violation and no
padding byte is read.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
EVERY ENUMERATOR IS REPRESENTABLE AS INT, WHICH THE STANDARD REQUIRES RATHER THAN MERELY
FAVOURS. C11 6.7.2.2p2 constrains the expression defining an enumeration constant to an integer
constant expression whose value is representable as an int, so an enumerator outside that range
is a constraint violation and not a portability preference: a conforming implementation must
diagnose it, and a program containing one is outside what a differential oracle can compare
because the two sides are free to differ on what they do after diagnosing. Every enumerator in
this program is therefore inside int range by construction. The extremes are exactly the int
minimum (B_LOW) and the int maximum (B_HIGH), and no other enumerator comes near either, so the
constraint is satisfied at both edges rather than merely somewhere in the middle.

THE ONE IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED RELATION THAT IS PRINTED, AND WHY PRINTING IT IS SOUND. No raw
enum size ever reaches stdout: the program prints sizeof(enum color) == sizeof(int) as a 0 or 1
value only. Even so, C11 6.7.2.2p4 leaves an enumerated type's compatible type
implementation-defined -- for values within int range exactly as much as beyond it -- so that
relation is an implementation-defined observation rather than a language guarantee, and printing it
needs a justification instead of an assumption.

Three things together supply one. First, it is a RELATION and not a width, so no byte count
enters the golden record and the measured i686 width difference cannot surface through it.
Second, it was MEASURED rather than assumed: compiling and running this comparison with each
target's own reference cross driver yields 1 on all four targets at all three optimization
levels -- twelve configurations, all agreeing, with sizeof(enum color) four bytes in every one.
Third, on these four targets the choice is not free in practice. Each is a hosted ELF target
whose objects must interoperate with a system C library built by that same reference compiler,
so the compatible type is fixed by the platform's established ABI rather than chosen afresh by
each compiler. Agreeing with the reference compiler on the same target is therefore an ABI
requirement and not a convention, and a disagreement would be an ABI incompatibility -- exactly
the class of defect this suite exists to surface.

This line is consequently treated as a MEASURED NON-DIFFERENCE, on the same footing as bitfield
layout, negative right shift, and division and remainder signs; see
tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md section 5.4. A divergence on it is a genuine finding
rather than an expected implementation-defined difference, and no marker covers it.

Should a future backend or a newly supported target ever legitimately choose a different
compatible type, the correct response is to delete this printed relation or to restrict this
program's target list with a recorded reason -- never to mint a marker and never to weaken an
oracle. Independently of all of the above, every enumerator is explicitly cast to int before
printing, which normalizes the printed values and keeps the %d conversion correct on every
target whatever compatible type is chosen.

REMAINING WIDTH NORMALIZATION. No sizeof of long, pointer, double, long long or any aggregate
containing them is printed, so the measured i686 difference (4-byte long and pointer versus 8
bytes on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64) cannot surface. No address or pointer value is printed.
Plain char is not used, so the measured char signedness difference (signed on x86_64 and i686,
unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) is irrelevant. The only printf conversion specifier used in
the program is %d.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
color=0 1 2
sparse=10 11 20 21
signed=-5 0 7 8
bounded_low_high=-2147483648 2147483647
arith=13 11
classify_folded=300 classify_runtime=300
enum_size_eq_int=1
END
